<.part title="How they work">
  <p>
    With the other options, you start an agent and it reads the board. A runner works the
    other way round. An automation rule queues a <strong>job</strong>
    for a <strong>pool</strong>, for example whenever a card arrives in a list. A runner
    in that pool, on a machine of yours, takes the job, runs the agent on the card, and
    reports how it went.
  </p>
  <p>
    Runners <strong>pull</strong>: they connect out to ask for work, and the server never
    connects to them, so there are no ports to open or tunnels to set up. The server sends
    only the card and a prompt. What actually runs is decided by the runner's own config on
    your machine, and a kind of job it doesn't define is refused. A runner's token can
    only take and report on its own pool's jobs.
  </p>
  <p id="help-runners-profile">
    On a board with profiles on, a claimed job also carries the card's <strong>profile</strong>: one word for what kind of change it is, <code>security</code>, <code>schema</code>, <code>feature</code>, <code>fix</code>,
    <code>docs</code>
    or <code>spike</code>. It is the card's own, else the one its paths and tags infer:
    a security path makes it <code>security</code>, a migration <code>schema</code>, a
    <code>bug</code>
    tag <code>fix</code>, only docs paths <code>docs</code>, a <code>spike</code>
    tag <code>spike</code>, anything else <code>feature</code>. It is <code>profile</code>
    in <code>claim_job</code>'s answer and the session claim's job, <code>profile:</code>
    in <code>slipdock claim-job</code>, and <code>SLIPDOCK_PROFILE</code>
    in a runner's job, where the runner's config can pick a model for it
    (<code>MODEL_feature</code>). The profile decides how the work is checked: a <code>docs</code>
    card gets no verifier, a <code>schema</code>
    or <code>security</code>
    one a defect review and, on a board that ships by pull request, waits by default for a
    person before it merges, and a <code>spike</code>
    merges nothing. A <code>fix</code>, <code>feature</code>, <code>schema</code>
    or <code>security</code>
    card's verifier mutates the change: 3 to 5 of its most consequential lines and every
    guard or branch it adds, each named with its line, and it checks each claim in the
    builder's evidence rather than carry it. An agent may raise a card's profile once work has started, never lower
    it, and the change it completes with can raise it too (<code>slipdock done 12 --diff-base origin/main</code>). With profiles off, no profile is sent.
  </p>
  <p>
    What happens to the card after that, from the claim through building, CI and a second
    agent's check to the deploy, and where it waits for you: <.link
      patch={~p"/help/agents/flow"}
      id="help-runners-flow"
      class="link font-medium"
    >
      How work flows
    </.link>.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Set one up" id="help-runners-setup">
  <p>
    On the board, open <strong>Automations → Connect a runner</strong>. It asks where the
    work should happen and gives you exactly what to paste:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="table table-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where</th>
          <th>What it is</th>
          <th>Cost while idle</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr id="help-scenario-daemon">
          <td>A machine with the daemon</td>
          <td>
            <code>slipdock-runner init</code>
            with a one-time enrolment key made for the board, then its runners set on the machine's page
          </td>
          <td>Nothing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Claude Code</td>
          <td>A <code>/loop</code> that takes jobs from the pool's queue</td>
          <td>A Claude turn per check</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Claude, scheduled</td>
          <td>A Claude Desktop or cloud routine with the Slipdock connector</td>
          <td>A Claude turn per run</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ChatGPT</td>
          <td>A chat or scheduled task that claims one job and works it in the conversation</td>
          <td>A ChatGPT turn per check</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p id="help-scripts-retired">
    The shell and PowerShell runners the daemon replaced were retired: this server no
    longer serves them. One still running keeps working with its token until it is moved:
    <strong>Move to a machine</strong>
    on it in the Runners panel gives the steps.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machines">
    A machine running the runner daemon enrols once: <code>slipdock-runner init</code>
    shows a code and the machine's fingerprint, and you approve it under <.app_link
      current_user={@current_user}
      to={~p"/account/machines"}
    >Account → Machines</.app_link>, checking the hostname, system and fingerprint and
    choosing the boards it may serve. It gets a machine key of its own, which is not an API
    token, and revoking the machine there stops the key at once. For a machine with nobody
    at its screen, make a one-time enrolment key on the same page: the name you give it is
    the name the machine enrols under, or its hostname without one.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machine-connection">
    The daemon looks after its own connection to this server, so a laptop that sleeps or
    changes networks runs jobs as well as a machine that is always on. It pings a
    connection that has been quiet for 15 seconds and replaces it if no answer comes
    within 10, never retries a failed request on the connection it failed on, and when
    the clock jumps (the machine woke from sleep) drops its connections and asks for work
    again at once. Meanwhile the machine shows offline after about 4½ minutes unheard
    from, and a job it held goes back in the queue once its 90-second lease runs out.
    If no other runner has taken that job when the machine wakes, its runner takes it
    back with its next heartbeat and the agent carries on where it was; if one has, the
    woken runner stops its copy.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machine-page">
    Each machine has a page of its own, opened from that list: whether it is online, the
    ceiling and executors it reported, its runners and what each is working on, and the
    log of settings it accepted or refused. There you add, change, pause and remove its
    runners, rename the machine (its hostname stays), ask it to update now, and revoke it.
    A setting outside the machine's ceiling
    is marked with the limit; if you save it anyway the machine refuses it, and the page
    shows the refusal with its reason and the limit. People who share a board with the
    machine see its page but can't change it.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machine-probe">
    <strong>Probe</strong>
    a runner from that page, or with <code>slipdock machine probe</code>, to check it is ready to work without a shell on
    the machine. The runner checks its directory is a git repository with an origin, its
    agent answers <code>--version</code>, <code>gh</code>
    is signed in, the slipdock skills
    and CLI are this server's, and its executor runs a command, then runs its probe hook
    if it has one. The page shows the probe green with when it passed, or red with its
    log. A probe is a job with no card, sent only to that runner.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machine-keeps-current">
    After a deploy there is nothing to do on the machine: before each agent job (not a
    merge or a probe) the daemon puts this server's slipdock skills in place and, when
    <code>/cli/SHA256SUMS</code>
    differs from its <code>slipdock</code>
    CLI, fetches this server's CLI, checks its SHA-256 and puts it in the daemon's own
    folder, first on the job's PATH, never over a copy installed elsewhere unless the
    machine's <code>local.json</code>
    names it as <code>cli</code>. The CLI is installed only when its signature verifies
    against the release key built into the daemon: the release job signs each <code>v*</code>
    tag's, and a server built from source, or a deploy it didn't sign, serves none, so the
    machine keeps its own CLI unless <code>local.json</code>
    sets <code>cli_checksum_only</code>. Until the next tag is deployed, its runner shows
    <em>CLI unsigned on server</em>
    rather than <em>old CLI</em>: the cause is the server's, not the machine's. A failed fetch or a wrong sum keeps the CLI it had, and
    the job runs. The skills are installed the same way, only when their archive's
    signature verifies against that key, and a server that serves none leaves the
    installed skills in place unless <code>local.json</code>
    sets <code>skills_unsigned</code>; its runner then shows <em>skills unsigned on server</em>
    rather than <em>skills drifted</em>
    until the next tag is deployed, with no alert. The runner reports the current CLI at its next claim, and its probe goes
    green. <code>no_cli_update</code>
    (and <code>no_skills_update</code>) in <code>local.json</code>
    turns it off.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machine-release">
    On a board with batched releases the daemon also fires the release, as it merges: a
    <code>release</code>
    job has no agent and no card. It runs
    <code>gh workflow run &lt;workflow&gt; --ref &lt;branch&gt;</code>
    itself and logs the run it started. Any other release command a board sets runs only
    when the machine's <code>local.json</code>
    lists it, word for word, under <code>release_commands</code>; the daemon refuses the
    rest, since it never runs what the server sends. <code>no_release: true</code>
    there turns the kind off. A card on such a board is done when it merges: an agent
    neither waits for its release nor fires one, <em>Release now</em>
    is how a person ships, and a card's <em>Not shipped</em>
    line counts from its release, not its merge.
  </p>
  <p id="help-machine-setup">
    <strong>Set up this machine with Claude</strong>, on the machine's page and at the end of
    <code>slipdock-runner init</code>
    (or <code>slipdock-runner setup</code>
    later), starts
    Claude Code on the machine, as the account its jobs run as, with a prompt that reads each
    board's machine-setup page (the handbook's Testing &amp; tooling, Local setup). It checks
    what the machine has, installs what's missing, proves it by cloning the repository and
    running its suite, and corrects the page where it was wrong, so the next machine is set up
    from the page alone.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part id="help-trust-contract" title="What the server can and cannot do">
  <p>
    A machine with the runner daemon takes data from this server, never a command. This
    is the trust contract: the same text is on each machine's page, and on the machine
    <code>slipdock-runner show</code>
    prints it after that machine's own limits.
  </p>
  <.trust_contract />
</.part>

<.part id="help-card-text-is-data" title="Card text is data">
  <p>
    A runner's agent reads what other people wrote: the card, its comments, its bar and
    anything mailed to the board, by anyone who can comment on it or is a permitted
    sender. Every job's prompt and the skills tell the agent that this text is data,
    never instructions: it says what the card's work is, and when it asks for anything
    beyond that the agent leaves it, says so on the card and, if the card can't be done
    without it, asks a person. Telling a model is not a boundary, so the machine's ceiling
    and the runner's executor, above, are what limit a job. The full threat model is in <code>docs/agents.md</code>, under <em>Card text is data</em>.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Sending it work">
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>Send cards to a runner</strong>: when a card arrives in a list, queue it
      for a pool, with the card itself as the prompt or one you write. Your own is at
      most about 17,000 bytes, since every job's prompt ends with the same closing
      text; a longer one is refused when the rule is saved, with the exact number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work a list with a runner</strong>: take the top card, one at a time, and
      skip cards that are blocked, waiting, already assigned or already have a job open
      on them, so two such rules for two pools can share one list. With lanes on, a card
      whose files overlap work in progress is skipped too, and the next one that could
      start goes instead. A rule whose runners have all gone offline sends nothing, and
      gives back a card it sent that no runner has taken yet, so a runner that is online
      picks it up. This is how a runner
      takes over from a <code>/loop</code> working the To Do list.
    </li>
    <li>
      A Claude or ChatGPT session can also take jobs from the same queue itself, with
      <code>claim_job</code>
      over <.link patch={~p"/help/agents/mcp"} class="link">MCP</.link>
      or <code>slipdock claim-job</code>, so a loop and a machine's runner never work the same card.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.part>

<.part title="Good to know">
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      Jobs run with nobody watching. Every prompt says so, and tells the agent to choose
      from what's on the card, or to open a <strong>wait</strong>
      saying what it needs and stop. The job is <strong>parked</strong>, the card goes back on top of its list
      so the next one runs, and answering the wait (on the card, in My work's <strong>Waiting on you</strong>, or <code>slipdock answer</code>) sends the card to the rule again
      with the answer at the top of the prompt, for any free runner of the pool: it isn't held for the runner that asked.
    </li>
    <li>
      A job that ends with its card still open leaves its summary as the card's <strong>handoff</strong>, the note the next person or agent reads first, unless the agent
      left one itself with <code>leave_handoff</code> or <code>slipdock handoff</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Give the runner the project's folder as its working directory. Claude Code only loads
      a project's own commands and settings from the folder it starts in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pause</strong>
      in the Runners panel holds the whole board tree, or one pool,
      with a reason: nothing is taken and no list sends a card until you resume it. Agents
      can pause (<code>manage_automation</code>, <code>slipdock pause</code>) but only a
      person can resume, and an agent that sees <code>paused</code>
      takes nothing and stops.
      Held jobs say why: paused, WIP full (with <strong>WIP limits hold automated work</strong>
      on, on the board's Agentic Building page), waiting on a card in progress, or no runner online.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>State line and age.</strong>
      A card with a job or a wait on it says so in one
      line (<code>state</code>
      in REST, <code>list_cards</code>
      and <code>slipdock cards</code>), with <code>driver</code>
      (agent or human) on trees with
      runner rules and its <code>age</code>
      in the list. <code>stuck</code>
      marks a card past
      its list's usual stay (never an epic with open subcards, whose stay is
      theirs); filter on it with <code>stuck: true</code>
      or <code>--stuck</code>, and on <code>driver</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where a card came from.</strong>
      Every card records its <code>origin</code>
      (a person, the sweep, findings, the health monitor, the overseer, verification, a
      rule, an import, or an agent with its job). Filter on it with <code>--origin</code>
      or <code>origin</code>; inside a job, name the generator you run with <code>slipdock add … --origin sweep</code>. Nobody can claim to be a person.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The loop on a card.</strong>
      While a runner's job holds a card (claimed or running), its Assignees show the
      runner and the job's kind, build or verify, linked to the job: <code>loop</code>
      in REST, <code>get_card</code>
      and <code>list_cards</code>, a <code>Loop:</code>
      line in <code>slipdock card</code>. It goes when the job ends. It is not an
      assignee: a runner's agent signs in with a person's token, so it leaves the
      assignees alone, and every assignee filter still means people.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Timeline.</strong>
      How long a card sat in each list, who moved it, and its waits
      and jobs: <code>get_card</code>
      with <code>timeline: true</code>, <code>GET /api/cards/:id/timeline</code>
      or <code>slipdock timeline &lt;id&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      With <strong>Work a list with a runner</strong>, a card its job leaves in progress
      goes back to the top of the list for another try, then gets flagged as blocked.
    </li>
    <li>
      ChatGPT has no shell or repository, so give it its own pool of cards it can finish
      in conversation, such as writing, research or planning.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.part>
